\subsection{How many comparisons carry a conclusion}\label{sec:headline}
Table~\ref{tab:headline} gives, for one dataset per design, the number of comparisons whose alteration reverses a conclusion, by statistical separation. In Arena-55k a pair with $z$ between 1 and 2 is reversed by a few dozen alterations, and by about 150 when $z$ is between 4 and 8, which is 0.06\% to 0.3\% of the votes; for NBA 2005 the same separations take about one in a hundred to three in a hundred of the games. At equal $z$ the share of the data differs by an order of magnitude between designs, so that raw shares cannot be compared across datasets; in the same table $Q$ is between 0.8 and 1.2 for all of them. The leaderboard's top place is the most fragile statement: for the top model of Arena-55k against the second, $z=\topArenaZ$ and \topArenaDelK{} deleted or \topArenaFlipK{} flipped vote reverses it; in \topNLow{} of the \topN{} datasets the top-1 and top-2 models are less than 2 standard errors apart.

