\paragraph{The scale.}
If the pairs of a dataset were as fragile as the same-graph Bradley--Terry null predicts, $Q$ would equal $Q_{\mathrm{null}}$, and fragility in standard-error units would be the property of the graph and of $z$ alone. That is what we find (Figure~\ref{fig:scale}): the real $k^{*}$ is close to the null's for separations $z\in[1,8]$ (A), and $Q_{\mathrm{real}}/Q_{\mathrm{null}}$ stays near one there for all designs (B). In \nEquiv{} of the \nDeltaRows{} (dataset, family) rows the whole 95\% interval of $\Delta$ lies inside $\pm\deltaMargin$ (a ratio of $1/\ratioMargin$ to $\ratioMargin$ on $Q$), none lies beyond the margin, and \nInconc{} are undecided because they rest on few pairs (\incList). $\Delta$ is reported for \nDeltaSets{} of the \nSets{} datasets: the unmatched MT-Bench GPT-4 set has four pairs in range, fewer than the five the rule needs, and its matched version, on the questions the experts also judged, is included. Equivalence is not identity: \nExZero{} intervals exclude zero, \nExZeroPos{} positive and \nExZeroNeg{} negative. The largest departures are outcome flips on the crowd and judge data of Arena, $\Delta=\arenaFlipDelta$ [\arenaFlipLo, \arenaFlipHi] for Arena-55k, that is $k^{*}$ about \arenaFlipRatio{} times the null's (a positive $\Delta$ means the real data need \emph{more} alterations than the null). Most is an offset of the method: simulated Arena-55k data with no departure (\arenaCtlNull{} null simulations) give $\arenaCtlFlip$ [\arenaCtlLo, \arenaCtlHi]; deletion is on the null for Arena-55k ($\Delta=\arenaDelDelta$ [\arenaDelLo, \arenaDelHi]). That $Q$ sits near 1 is largely the first-order identity $k^{*}g_{\max}\approx\Delta$ of Section~\ref{sec:method}; it makes $Q$ a gauge, and the statement above is about $\Delta$.

