\subsection{Design or statistics}\label{sec:design}
The same-graph comparison asks whether a dataset is more or less fragile than its own statistics imply; whether the \emph{labeller} matters is a question about two labellings of identical items. Figure~\ref{fig:design} shows $\Delta$ for every dataset by design (A) and the paired difference of $\Delta$ between each LLM judge and the crowd on the identical Arena battles (B; \matchedBoot{} joint resamples of battles, \matchedPairs{} $z$-stratified pairs per member and family). All \nPairedEquiv{} of the \nPairedRows{} paired differences of the \nMatchedJudges{} judge contrasts are equivalent within the margin; \nPairedExZero{} of them exclude zero, none with a point difference above \pairedExZeroAbsMax{} on the log scale (interval ends reach \pairedExZeroEndMax{}). On MT-Bench, experts against GPT-4 on the identical questions, with only \mtMatchedPairs{} pairs in range, outcome flips are equivalent ($\mtPairedFlip$ [$\mtPairedFlipLo$, $\mtPairedFlipHi$]) and deletion is undecided ($\mtPairedDel$ [$\mtPairedDelLo$, $\mtPairedDelHi$]). In every design the count per unit of separation lies within $\pm\deltaMargin$ of the reference, but $\Delta$ is not a test of misfit: on data simulated from the MT-Bench human graph with a departure added (overdispersion up to a design effect of \ctlDesEffMax{}, position bias, a cyclic or a pair-specific component), $\Delta$ stayed within the margin at every size tried, the largest $|\Delta|$ being \ctlDeltaMax{} (Appendix~\ref{app:control}). The sizes were fixed in advance, not matched to real departures, and none reached the margin. A departure of the interval rather than of the count does appear: the median ratio of the cluster-bootstrap to the model-based standard error of a gap is \seRatioExpert{} for MT-Bench expert labels (design effect \seDesEffExpert{} from the questions), against \seNullExpert{} for the same-graph simulation of that dataset. The ratio is \seRatioCrowd{} for Arena crowd votes against \seNullCrowd{} in simulation, an effect of ties, and \seRatioJudge{} and \seRatioSports{} for the LLM judges and the NBA seasons, as in simulation.

