\subsection{Predicting $k^{*}$ without searching}\label{sec:rule}
A regression of $\log k^{*}$ on $\log z$, $\log N$, $\log$ density and tie rate ($\nFitPairsDel{}$ pairs, \nUnitsDel{} datasets; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:rule}) gives $\coefZDel$ [\coefZDelLo, \coefZDelHi] on $\log z$ and $\coefNDel$ [\coefNDelLo, \coefNDelHi] on $\log N$ for deletion ($\coefZFlip$ and $\coefNFlip$ for flips; the $N$ coefficient conditions on $z$ and is not the equal-$N$ exponent above). Leaving one dataset out, the rule predicts $\log k^{*}$ with a root-mean-square error of \refRmseBaseDel{} (deletion) and \refRmseBaseFlip{} (flip) over \nHeldAll{} held-out datasets, a factor of \refFBaseDel{} and \refFBaseFlip. Against simpler predictors on the same units: the mean alone gives \refRmseIntDel{} and \refRmseIntFlip, $\log z$ alone \refRmseLogZDel{} and \refRmseLogZFlip, so $N$, density and ties matter. Reading the dataset's own same-graph null at the pair's $z$ (a simulation of the dataset, no search on the real data) is as good for deletion (\refRmseNullMeanDel; the gain of the regression in squared error is $\nullMeanGainDel$ [$\nullMeanGainDelLo$, $\nullMeanGainDelHi$]) and slightly worse for flips (\refRmseNullMeanFlip; gain $\nullMeanGainFlip$ [$\nullMeanGainFlipLo$, $\nullMeanGainFlipHi$]). The first-order estimate rescaled by its median bias on the other datasets (an influence computation, no refit) is worse for deletion (\refRmseFirstDel) and better for flips (\refRmseFirstFlip; gain of the regression $\firstGainFlip$ [$\firstGainFlipLo$, $\firstGainFlipHi$]). The regression is therefore not the best predictor of $k^{*}$; what it shows is that separation, size, density and ties explain $k^{*}$ about as well as a simulation of the dataset does. A design term lowers the held-out squared error by only \gainDel{} [\gainDelLo, \gainDelHi] for deletion and \gainFlip{} [\gainFlipLo, \gainFlipHi] for flips (the expert dataset is the only one of its design and is not held out for this term). Leaving a whole \emph{design} out is worse: the error is \rmseOutMinDel{} to \rmseOutMaxDel{} for deletion (largest for LLM judges, a factor of \rmseOutMaxFDel) and \rmseOutMinFlip{} to \rmseOutMaxFlip{} for flips. The rule transfers between datasets of a design and less well between designs (the held-out design is one or two datasets).

\subsection{Is the search good enough}\label{sec:search}
The reported $k^{*}$ is only as good as its search. Table~\ref{tab:search} compares methods on the same \nSearchConcl{} sampled conclusions per family (a $z$-spread sample for the two Arena datasets and all pairs of the two MT-Bench datasets), with the smallest verified $k^{*}$ found by any of them as the reference. At the budget the iterated search needs, one-shot influence ranking (the top-$k$ single-alteration effects, as in influence-function sensitivity analysis) reverses only \oneshotAtKDel\% of the deletion conclusions and \oneshotAtKFlip\% of the flip conclusions (\oneshotMinDel--\oneshotMaxDel\% and \oneshotMinFlip--\oneshotMaxFlip\% by dataset), so that the refit-and-relinearise step is needed; a residual heuristic needs \residRatioDel{} to \residRatioFlip{} times as many alterations, and fails to reverse \residUnreachDel\% (deletion) and \residUnreachFlip\% (flip) of the conclusions within its cap (all harmful alterations, or 50 times the iterated $k^{*}$). The first-order estimate without refit overshoots deletion by a factor of \linRatioDel{} and undershoots flips (\linRatioFlip). Where the minimum can be computed exactly (\nTinyExact{} tiny MT-Bench subproblems, \nTinyDel{} deletion and \nTinyFlip{} flip), the iterated search finds it in every case. At the scale of Arena only upper bounds are verified: 200 randomised restarts found a smaller count than the standard search on \restartsBetterDel\% (deletion) and \restartsBetterFlip\% (flip) of \nRestarts{} Arena-55k conclusions, so the reported $k^{*}$ is an upper bound with some slack, and on \nPlanted{} planted instances (a known set of alterations that undoes the conclusion) the search never needed more alterations than the planted path. An uncertified lower-bound candidate from a relaxation lies between \lpMin{} and \lpMax{} of the verified value (median per dataset). Counting a win turned into a loss as two alterations multiplies $k^{*}$ by \costTwoMin{} to \costTwoMax{}.

